# DTF tier manifest: auth-logout-revokes-session  (artifact-keeper#4831; 1.12.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. No extra services.
#
# THE BEHAVIOUR
# POST /api/v1/auth/logout must end the presenting session server-side: the
# session's refresh token (and its rotation lineage, the refresh-token family)
# stops minting access tokens. Before the fix the handler revoked the family
# only when the refresh token itself was in the request (JSON body
# `refresh_token`, or the `ak_refresh_token` cookie). Two real logout shapes
# carry neither:
#   A  API/CLI: `Authorization: Bearer <access>` + body `{}`
#   D  browser: the cookie jar the login set (the refresh cookie is scoped to
#      /api/v1/auth/refresh, so only `ak_access_token` reaches /logout), no body
# Both answered 200 while the refresh token kept working. The fix stamps the
# session's family id on the access token and logout revokes that family.
#
# THE GATES
#   (FIX)      shapes A and D: after a 200 logout, POST /auth/refresh with the
#              session's refresh token is 401 with no access_token in the body,
#              and every refresh_token_jti row of that family is revoked
#              (psql). RED on every release up to 1.11.3 (refresh 200, rows
#              unrevoked).
#   (CONTROL)  both images: shape B (bearer + refresh token in the body) and
#              shape C (bearer + `Cookie: ak_refresh_token`) revoke; replaying
#              a rotated refresh token is 401; logging out one session leaves
#              a second session of the same user refreshing (per-session
#              scoping, #1807).
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: seven logins and a dozen refreshes back-to-back.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
